  "run_log": [
    {"step": "registry", "rung": null, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 2, "searches": 0, "detail": "Registry route CONFIRMED and materially improved. brockton.gov is WordPress and the REST API works via WebFetch: wp-json/wp/v2/media?search=<term>&per_page=100&_fields=date,source_url,title returned the complete elections-document tree back to 2003, including files NOT linked from the elections-commission page (notably Nov-2019-Unofficial-Results.pdf, which the visible page omits). IMPORTANT CORRECTION: curl is 403-blocked (nginx) on brockton.gov for wp-json entirely and for many /wp-content/uploads paths, while WebFetch succeeds on all of them. The registry says the route is 'fetchable'; it is fetchable by WebFetch, not reliably by curl. Some upload paths did serve to curl (2018/11, 2021/10, 2023/10, 2023/11, 2024/06, 2025/12) while others 403'd persistently (2019/09, 2019/11, 2021/11, 2025/09, 2022/10, 2023/07) - no discernible pattern, so try both tools per file."},
    {"step": "cycle_calendar", "rung": null, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 3, "searches": 0, "detail": "Cadence confirmed from source, not assumed: biennial city election, first Tuesday after first Monday of November in odd years. All 12 offices (Mayor, 4 Councillors At Large, 7 Ward Councillors) run concurrently - no stagger. Verified 2017-11-07, 2019-11-05, 2021-11-02, 2023-11-07, 2025-11-04; each is a Tuesday (day-of-week checked). The 2017 cycle was retrieved because it seats the council serving calendar 2019 - the panel's first year is a HOLDOVER year with no election. Clerk archive bundles state/federal races (Sept/Nov 2020, 2022, 2024, March 2024) in the same folders; filtered by contest name as instructed. Massachusetts has no unopposed-candidate omission rule of the Alabama/Georgia type: unopposed Brockton seats DO appear on the ballot and in the results (e.g. Ward 2 2019 Monahan alone, Ward 5 2021 Thompson alone, Ward 2 2023 Tavares alone), so an absent seat here would be genuine absence."},
    {"step": "cycle_retrieval", "rung": 1, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 20, "searches": 0, "detail": "All five cycles sourced from the Brockton Elections Commission's own PDFs. 2017 and 2023 are born-digital: extracted with pdftotext -bbox and candidate columns matched to the TOTALS row by x-coordinate, because plain -layout reverses the reading order of the rotated candidate labels (it would have inverted the 2017 Ward 4 and Ward 6 winners). 2019 and 2021 are Dominion 'Election Summary Report' scans (CCITT Fax / JBIG2); WebFetch refused them but saved the binaries to disk as the brief predicts, and they were read by rendering to PNG with pdftoppm and reading the images directly - tesseract OCR was tried first and was unusable. 2025 is a hand-annotated scan whose vertical candidate labels defeat both pdftotext and OCR; it was read by rotating the rendered pages 90 degrees and reading them as images, which recovered the Mayor and Councillor-At-Large pages cleanly."},
    {"step": "validation", "rung": null, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 3, "searches": 1, "detail": "Two independent official 'ELECTED CITY OFFICIALS' rosters were found on the same WordPress site and are OVERWRITTEN IN PLACE, so their upload paths lie about their vintage - a trap worth recording. uploads/2020/01/ELECTED-CITY-OFFICIALS-1.pdf (media date 2024-11-21) actually carries the 2024-2025 roster, and uploads/2024/06/ELECTED-CITY-OFFICIALS.pdf (media date 2026-01-09) carries the 2026 roster. Both were extracted verbatim with pdftotext -layout and both match the election results name-for-name, independently corroborating the 2024-2025 and 2026 councils. The 2026 roster additionally supplied the seven ward winners for 2025, which the 2025 results scan could not yield. Council size constant at 12 elective offices (mayor + 4 at-large + 7 wards) across the whole panel; the 11-member council was confirmed by roll-call arithmetic in the June 26 2023 minutes (10 present + 1 absent)."},
    {"step": "midterm_check", "rung": 4, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 8, "searches": 3, "detail": "ONE mid-term change found and dated: Mayor Bill Carpenter died in office 2019-07-03; Council President Moises M. Rodrigues succeeded him and was then elected by his council colleagues to serve out the remainder of the term, to 2020-01-06 (WGBH, Boston Globe, CBS Boston). Rodrigues did not run for mayor in 2019 - Robert F. Sullivan won that race - and Rodrigues returned to an at-large seat in January 2020. He was elected mayor outright in 2025 and sworn in 2026-01-05 (a Monday, checked). TWO suspected mid-term changes were tested and DISPROVED: (a) Rita Mendes, at-large, was sworn as state representative for the 11th Plymouth district on 2023-01-04, which looked like a resignation, but the Enterprise reports she simply 'did not run for reelection to the at-large seat', and the June 26 2023 council minutes record a full 11-member body, so she is recorded as serving the full 2022-2024 term at medium confidence; (b) Ward 3's Mark D'Agostino, elected 2021 and absent from the 2023 ballot, looked like a mid-term resignation, but the June 26 2023 minutes name him verbatim ('Councilor D'Agostino absent', plus 20 further mentions), proving he sat to the end of the term and simply did not seek re-election. Ward 1 2025 was decided by FIVE votes (1033-1028) and went to a recount continued to December 10, 2025; the recounted result is the source used."},
    {"step": "escalation", "rung": 6, "outcome": "success", "tool_calls": 21, "searches": 0, "detail": "SECOND PASS, +18 calls, 0 searches. The presiding officer is now sourced for EVERY panel year, and the sole remaining gap is narrowed. The ACFR route (cross-state finding 3c) was tried FIRST and is DEAD for this city: a wp-json media search for 'annual comprehensive financial' returns an empty array, so no ACFR series is hosted on the city domain at all. Rung 6 (minutes roll call) closed it instead, and the WP media REST endpoint is what made it cheap - search=City_Council_Minutes_01 returned every January meeting 2015-2025 in a single call. KEY STRUCTURAL FINDING: Brockton elects its Council President ANNUALLY, not once per two-year term, so each January meeting carries a nominations-and-gavel-exchange passage naming BOTH the incoming and the OUTGOING president - one document sources two years. Verbatim: 2020-01-13 'President Asack presiding' plus 'congratulated President Asack for doing a fine job in her new role'; 2021-01-11 'nominated Councilor Farwell as the next Council President for 2021 ... Councillor Farwell was elected by a roll call vote'; 2023-01-11 'Councilor Susan J. Nicastro is elected President' and 'President Nicastro presented outgoing President Lally with a plaque', which sources Lally for 2022 retrospectively; 2025-01-13 Jeffrey Thompson of Ward 5 elected 10-1 with 'Councilor Derenoncourt in the negative', and 'President Thompson thanked Councilor Rodrigues for his leadership as President', which sources Rodrigues for 2024 retrospectively. The January 2022 and January 2024 minutes do NOT contain the election - it happens at a separate Inauguration Ceremony (the 2022-01-10 minutes accept 'Minutes of the January 3, 2022 Inauguration Ceremony'), which is exactly why those two years had to be recovered from the FOLLOWING January's gavel-exchange passage. Date caution applied: the 2022-01-10 minutes carry an internal typo, 'held on Monday, January 10, 2021', in a document headed 'In City Council January 10, 2022'; January 10 2022 was a Monday and January 10 2021 was a Sunday, so the year in the body is the error. A THIRD mid-term change was also found and dated: the presidency changed hands mid-2019 as a knock-on of Carpenter's death - the 2019-10-15 minutes read 'Councillor Sullivan presiding' and later 'Councillor Sullivan stepped down as President and Councillor Cruise stepped in as President Pro Tempe', establishing Robert F. Sullivan as Council President for the balance of 2019; his start date is a soft boundary, so that row is medium confidence. That same roll call narrowed the surviving at-large gap: eleven seated members in October 2019 proves the vacated seat was FILLED, not left empty, so it is not a lawful vacancy but an unnamed appointee. Day-of-week check passed on the minutes' own header: 'held on Tuesday October 15, 2019' - a Tuesday. SUPERSEDED TEXT FOLLOWS, retained for the trail: The presiding officer could not be sourced for 2020-2025. Brockton's Council President is an internal office elected by the councillors and appears in no election record. The city council page names only the incumbent (John Lally, Ward 6). Council minutes were pursued as the rung-6 route: the WordPress media archive holds City Council minutes only from mid-2022 onward, and the two retrieved (2022-10-24, 2023-06-26) record attendance without naming the chair - a January organizational meeting would be needed and none was reachable before the cap. The ACFR route (cross-state finding 3c) was NOT attempted: budget was exhausted by the four-cycle workload plus three image-only scans. It remains the best next lead for both the Council President series and the 2019 at-large vacancy."},
    {"step": "export", "rung": null, "outcome": "budget_cap", "tool_calls": 1, "searches": 0, "detail": "Written at the 60-call cap, then reopened for an 18-call second pass that closed the Council President series. Council President rows are dated to the January meeting at which the gavel actually changed hands, which is why they do not align exactly with the term boundaries. Term boundary dates are the first Monday of January (2018-01-01, 2020-01-06, 2022-01-03, 2024-01-01, 2026-01-05); only the 2026-01-05 inauguration is directly sourced (GBH, 2026-01-05, a Monday). The other four are the charter-implied first Monday and should be treated as soft boundaries - this is a caveat on sourced rows, not a gap. At-large seats in Brockton are NOT numbered or districted, so all four carry the identical seat_label 'At-Large'; four rows per term is correct, not a duplicate."}
